# Every setting HarnessSetup asks for. One line per setting, fields separated by '|':
#
#   KEY | SCOPE | LEVEL | TYPE | DEFAULT | WHEN | PROMPT | HELP
#
#   SCOPE   both | laptop | desktop     which machine's wizard asks for it
#   LEVEL   basic | advanced            advanced ones are asked only on request (--advanced)
#   TYPE    ip cidr port int:MIN-MAX bool01 unixuser ghname repos noreply model optmodel gguf url time
#           winpath alias choice:a,b,c      (validated before anything is written)
#   DEFAULT literal text, `auto:detector=fallback`, or text with {OTHER_KEY} (derived from that setting)
#   WHEN    empty, or KEY=VALUE: only ask when that setting has that value
#   PROMPT  one short line (no '|')      HELP  explanation shown above the prompt (may contain '|')
#
# `#> Title` starts a group. Order matters: a setting must come after the ones its DEFAULT refers to.
# Read by lib/config.sh (bash) and desktop/windows/Common.ps1 (PowerShell): keep it plain text.

#> Machines and network
OFFLINE|laptop|basic|bool01|0||Work without the internet (no GitHub, no OpenRouter)|The node still needs the internet once to install (Debian packages, Hermes, llama.cpp, the models: ~40GB). Afterwards, yes here means: stages 05, 07 and 12 are skipped, the default profile and the fallback chain keep no OpenRouter entry and the node ends in the local profile (its own models only), and stage 13 leaves ports 80 and 443 closed. Finished work still goes to the SMB share. Set it back to no and re-run stages 05, 07, 11, 12 and 13 to work online again.
LAPTOP_IP|both|basic|ip|auto:laptop_ip=192.168.1.150||Laptop IP address|The laptop's address on your LAN. Give it a DHCP reservation on the router so it never changes (guide Step 1).
DESKTOP_IP|both|basic|ip|auto:desktop_ip=192.168.1.100||Desktop IP address|The Windows desktop's address (also reserved on the router). The desktop serves the stronger local model.
ROUTER_IP|both|basic|ip|auto:router_ip=192.168.1.1||Router / DNS address|Your router. Once the firewall is on, the laptop may ask DNS only of this address (guide Step 28).
LAN_CIDR|laptop|basic|cidr|auto:lan_cidr=192.168.1.0/24||LAN network (CIDR)|Your home network, for example 192.168.1.0/24. The firewall stops the laptop reaching everything else on it.
ADMIN_USER|both|basic|unixuser|auto:admin_user=ai-node||Laptop admin account|The account you created in the Debian installer on the laptop (it has sudo). SSH from the desktop uses it.
AGENT_USER|laptop|advanced|unixuser|hermes||Agent account name|The dedicated account the agent runs as. It gets passwordless sudo (guide Step 6).
SSH_ALLOWED_FROM|laptop|advanced|ip|{DESKTOP_IP}||SSH allowed only from|Once the firewall is on, only this address may SSH into the laptop. Normally the desktop.
DASHBOARD_FROM|laptop|advanced|iplist|none||Devices that may open the dashboard in a browser (phone, another laptop)|none keeps the dashboard local-only (the desktop reaches it through the SSH tunnel). Otherwise their addresses / networks separated by commas (give them DHCP reservations): the dashboard then listens on the LAN with a password (stage 08 asks for it) and stage 13 lets only these devices in.
SMB_SHARE|laptop|advanced|smbpath|none||Network share for finished work|none, or a share like //192.168.1.20/work. ./setup.sh tool smb-share mounts it for the agent at /srv/share (it asks for the share's user and password) and stage 13 lets the laptop reach it.
DASHBOARD_PORT|both|advanced|port|9119||Dashboard port|Port of the Hermes dashboard. It is used on BOTH ends of the SSH tunnel, because the dashboard rejects other Host headers.
LLM_PORT|both|advanced|port|8080||Model server port|Port the llama.cpp servers listen on (laptop: loopback only; desktop: LAN, reachable from the laptop only).

#> GitHub (guide Step 8)
GITHUB_ORG|laptop|basic|ghname|||GitHub organisation or user|The GitHub account that owns the repos the agent will work on.
GITHUB_REPOS|laptop|basic|repos|||Repositories (space-separated)|Repo names only, e.g. "api web". The agent clones them all; scheduled jobs use the first one.
GITHUB_MACHINE_USER|laptop|basic|ghname|{GITHUB_ORG}-hermes|OFFLINE=0|Machine account username|The separate GitHub account that owns the fine-grained token (so you can review and approve the agent's PRs). It needs Write access to the repos.
GITHUB_NOREPLY_EMAIL|laptop|basic|noreply||OFFLINE=0|Machine account noreply email|Machine account > Settings > Emails, shaped like 12345678+yourorg-hermes@users.noreply.github.com. Commits use it.

#> OpenRouter models (guide Steps 10, 11, 21)
OR_WORKER_MODEL|laptop|basic|model||OFFLINE=0|Worker model (OpenRouter ID)|Strong mid-tier agentic coder for the subagents, which write most of the code. Pick the exact ID from the live list in 'hermes model' (names change often).
OR_REVIEW_MODEL|laptop|basic|model||OFFLINE=0|Reviewer model (OpenRouter ID)|A frontier model from a DIFFERENT family than your planner, so it does not share the planner's blind spots.
OR_COMPRESSION_MODEL|laptop|basic|model||OFFLINE=0|Summariser model (OpenRouter ID)|A capable mid-tier model that compresses long sessions. A weak summariser loses detail.
OR_FALLBACK_MODEL|laptop|basic|model|||Fallback model (OpenRouter ID)|Used when the planner fails. Pick one from a different upstream company than the planner.
CRON_MODEL|laptop|advanced|optmodel||OFFLINE=0|Cheaper model for scheduled jobs|Optional. Runs unpinned cron jobs on a cheaper model without changing your chat model. Enter - for none.
REASONING_EFFORT|laptop|advanced|choice:low,medium,high,xhigh,max|high|OFFLINE=0|Planner reasoning effort|High costs more tokens but thinks harder. Lower it (medium) if cost or latency outgrows the gain.
MAX_CONCURRENT_CHILDREN|laptop|advanced|int:1-10|3|OFFLINE=0|Parallel subagents|3 keeps spend predictable; Hermes' own default is 10.
MAX_ITERATIONS|laptop|advanced|int:10-1000|200|OFFLINE=0|Max iterations per subagent|Upper bound on a subagent's tool-call loop.
APPROVAL_MODE|laptop|advanced|choice:off,smart,manual|off||Dangerous-command approval mode|off (the guide's design): the agent, its sub-agents and its cron jobs run every command, sudo included, without asking, so unattended work never stalls. smart: a guardian model asks first, and sub-agents and cron jobs then refuse risky commands instead of waiting. manual: always ask. A short hard-coded blocklist (rm -rf / and the like) applies in every mode.
APPROVAL_DENY|laptop|advanced|bool01|1||Block destructive commands even with approvals off|A short list of command patterns Hermes refuses in every approval mode, sub-agents and cron included: deleting or archiving a GitHub repo, formatting or overwriting a disk, deleting the agent's own state or repos, switching the firewall off. Everything else stays allowed. See docs/RUNBOOK.md for the list.
WORKER_EFFORT|laptop|advanced|choice:inherit,low,medium,high,xhigh|inherit|OFFLINE=0|Sub-agent reasoning effort|inherit: sub-agents think as hard as the planner (REASONING_EFFORT). medium saves OpenRouter spend on routine pieces of work; high and above cost more.
AGENT_SUDO|laptop|advanced|choice:full,limited,none|full||What the agent may run as root|full (the guide's design): passwordless sudo for everything. limited: passwordless only apt, apt-get, systemctl and journalctl (installing packages and managing services); this reduces accidents but is not a security boundary, since a package install runs scripts as root. none: no sudo at all. Stage 03 applies it.
JOURNAL_MAX_MB|laptop|advanced|int:100-20000|500||System log size limit (MB)|The laptop's system log (all services: the model server, Hermes's gateway and dashboard) is capped at this size and a month of history, so an always-on node never fills its disk with logs. Hermes rotates its own log files.
SPEND_WARN_PCT|laptop|advanced|int:10-100|80||Warn at this share of the OpenRouter credit limit (%)|./setup.sh tool spend and verify warn when the key has used this much of its limit, and say so when the key has no limit or a limit that never resets.
BACKUP_KEEP|both|advanced|int:1-365|14||Backups to keep|How many daily backups of the agent's state (tools/backup.sh) the laptop keeps, and the desktop keeps of its copies (Backup-Laptop.ps1).
BACKUP_TIME|both|advanced|time|07:15||Daily backup time (laptop)|When the laptop backs up the agent's state; after the overnight window, so no night job is writing. The desktop copies it half an hour later.
DESKTOP_BACKUP_DIR|desktop|advanced|winpath|C:\hermes-backups||Folder for the laptop's backups (desktop)|Backup-Laptop.ps1 copies the newest laptop backup here. The folder is made readable by you and administrators only: the backups hold API keys.
AUTO_AWAY_GPU_PCT|desktop|advanced|int:5-100|25||GPU use that means you are using the desktop (%)|Auto-Away.ps1 takes the desktop out of Hermes's loop when other programs (games, video, 3D work) keep the GPU at least this busy. The model servers themselves do not count.
AUTO_AWAY_AFTER_MIN|desktop|advanced|int:1-60|2||Minutes of your GPU use before the models step aside|How long other programs must keep the GPU busy before Auto-Away.ps1 stops the model servers (as Desktop-Mode.ps1 away).
AUTO_BACK_AFTER_MIN|desktop|advanced|int:5-240|15||Quiet minutes before the models come back|After an automatic away: how long the GPU must stay quiet before Auto-Away.ps1 starts the model servers again (as Desktop-Mode.ps1 back).

#> Laptop model: Qwen3.5-9B on the GTX 1070 (guide Step 18)
LAPTOP_QUANT|laptop|basic|choice:UD-Q5_K_XL,UD-Q4_K_XL|UD-Q5_K_XL||Laptop model quantization|UD-Q5_K_XL is the guide's pick (6.7GB, higher quality). Choose UD-Q4_K_XL (6.0GB) if Q5 runs out of VRAM.
LAPTOP_MODEL_ALIAS|laptop|advanced|alias|qwen3.5-9b||Laptop model name|The name the server reports and Hermes uses.
LAPTOP_MODEL_FILE|laptop|advanced|gguf|Qwen3.5-9B-{LAPTOP_QUANT}.gguf||Laptop model file|File name in /srv/models. Derived from the quantization unless you override it.
LAPTOP_MODEL_URL|laptop|advanced|url|https://huggingface.co/unsloth/Qwen3.5-9B-GGUF/resolve/main/Qwen3.5-9B-{LAPTOP_QUANT}.gguf||Laptop model download URL|Check the exact file name on the Hugging Face repo's Files tab if it 404s.
LAPTOP_CTX|laptop|advanced|int:65536-1048576|131072||Laptop context size (tokens)|Hermes needs at least 65536 (64K); Qwen advises 131072 (128K) to keep reasoning intact.
LAPTOP_KV_IN_RAM|laptop|advanced|bool01|1||Keep the laptop context cache in RAM|Yes keeps the 8GB of VRAM for the weights (the guide's layout). Say no only if the Step 18 benchmark favours it.
LAPTOP_CACHE_RAM_MB|laptop|advanced|int:256-8192|2048||Laptop prompt cache (MB of RAM)|Keeps recent prompts warm. The llama.cpp default of 8GB is too much for 16GB of RAM.
LAPTOP_CHAT_KWARGS|laptop|advanced|kwargs|enable_thinking=true||Laptop chat-template switches|Chat-template switches as key=value pairs separated by commas (no spaces), sent to the model's template on every request (llama-server --chat-template-kwargs). 'none' sends nothing; 'auto' uses the kit's value for the Qwen model it ships. Change it only with the model (see docs/MODELS.md). Qwen3.5's small models think only when asked, so the kit asks.
LAPTOP_SAMPLING|laptop|advanced|sampling|--temp 0.6 --top-p 0.95 --top-k 20 --min-p 0 --presence-penalty 0||Laptop sampling|llama-server sampling flags, e.g. --temp 0.6 --top-p 0.95 (allowed: --temp --top-p --top-k --min-p --presence-penalty --frequency-penalty --repeat-penalty --repeat-last-n --typical --top-nsigma --xtc-probability --xtc-threshold --dry-multiplier --dry-base --dry-allowed-length --dry-penalty-last-n). 'none' leaves the server defaults; 'auto' uses the kit's values for the Qwen model it ships. Use the model card's recommended values (see docs/MODELS.md).

#> Desktop model: Qwen3.6-35B-A3B on the RX 6600 XT (guide Step 19)
DESKTOP_QUANT|both|basic|choice:UD-Q5_K_XL,UD-Q4_K_XL|UD-Q5_K_XL||Desktop model quantization|UD-Q5_K_XL (26.6GB) protects tool calling. Choose UD-Q4_K_XL (22.4GB) if Windows memory runs above about 90% while generating.
DESKTOP_MODEL_ALIAS|both|advanced|alias|qwen3.6-35b-a3b||Desktop model name|The name the desktop server reports and Hermes uses.
DESKTOP_MODEL_FILE|both|advanced|gguf|Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-{DESKTOP_QUANT}.gguf||Desktop model file|Derived from the quantization unless you override it.
DESKTOP_MODEL_URL|both|advanced|url|https://huggingface.co/unsloth/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF/resolve/main/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-{DESKTOP_QUANT}.gguf||Desktop model download URL|Check the exact file name on the Hugging Face repo's Files tab if it 404s.
DESKTOP_CTX|both|advanced|int:65536-1048576|131072||Desktop context size (tokens)|Same rule as the laptop: at least 65536, 131072 advised.
DESKTOP_N_CPU_MOE|desktop|advanced|int:0-64|40||Expert layers kept in RAM (--n-cpu-moe)|40 keeps all experts in RAM (safe start). Lower it until dedicated GPU memory sits near 7.3GB; each layer moved back adds about 0.6GB at Q5.
DESKTOP_CACHE_RAM_MB|desktop|advanced|int:256-8192|1024||Desktop prompt cache (MB of RAM)|Keeps recent prompts warm in RAM.
DESKTOP_CHAT_KWARGS|desktop|advanced|kwargs|preserve_thinking=true||Desktop chat-template switches|Chat-template switches as key=value pairs separated by commas (no spaces), sent to the model's template on every request (llama-server --chat-template-kwargs). 'none' sends nothing; 'auto' uses the kit's value for the Qwen model it ships. Change it only with the model (see docs/MODELS.md). Qwen3.6 keeps its earlier reasoning in context with preserve_thinking.
DESKTOP_SAMPLING|desktop|advanced|sampling|--temp 0.6 --top-p 0.95 --top-k 20 --min-p 0 --presence-penalty 0||Desktop sampling|llama-server sampling flags, e.g. --temp 0.6 --top-p 0.95 (allowed: --temp --top-p --top-k --min-p --presence-penalty --frequency-penalty --repeat-penalty --repeat-last-n --typical --top-nsigma --xtc-probability --xtc-threshold --dry-multiplier --dry-base --dry-allowed-length --dry-penalty-last-n). 'none' leaves the server defaults; 'auto' uses the kit's values for the Qwen model it ships. Use the model card's recommended values (see docs/MODELS.md).
DESKTOP_LLAMA_DIR|desktop|advanced|winpath|C:\llama||Desktop llama.cpp folder|Where the Vulkan build and start scripts live.
DESKTOP_MODELS_DIR|desktop|advanced|winpath|C:\models||Desktop models folder|Where the GGUF files are stored (the 35B needs about 27GB, the 27B about 17GB).

#> Overnight quality tier: Qwen3.8-27B (guide Step 31, optional)
NIGHT_ENABLED|both|basic|bool01|0||Set up the overnight Qwen3.8-27B tier|Optional. The desktop swaps to the dense 27B between two times, for unattended jobs only. It is too slow for interactive use.
NIGHT_START|both|basic|time|01:00|NIGHT_ENABLED=1|Overnight tier starts at (HH:MM)|When the 27B takes over. The desktop is woken from sleep for it.
NIGHT_END|both|basic|time|07:00|NIGHT_ENABLED=1|Overnight tier ends at (HH:MM)|When the normal model returns. Overnight jobs must finish before this.
NIGHT_MODEL_ALIAS|both|advanced|alias|qwen3.8-27b|NIGHT_ENABLED=1|Overnight model name|The name the 27B server reports and Hermes uses.
NIGHT_MODEL_FILE|desktop|advanced|gguf|Qwen3.8-27B-UD-Q4_K_XL.gguf|NIGHT_ENABLED=1|Overnight model file|File name in the desktop models folder.
NIGHT_MODEL_URL|desktop|advanced|url|https://huggingface.co/unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF/resolve/main/Qwen3.8-27B-UD-Q4_K_XL.gguf|NIGHT_ENABLED=1|Overnight model download URL|Check the exact file name on the Hugging Face repo's Files tab if it 404s.
NIGHT_NGL|desktop|advanced|int:0-99|16|NIGHT_ENABLED=1|GPU layers for the 27B (-ngl)|Start at 16 and raise it until dedicated GPU memory sits near 7.3GB (lower it if the server runs out of memory), or use llama-fit-params.
NIGHT_CHAT_KWARGS|desktop|advanced|kwargs|reasoning_effort=medium|NIGHT_ENABLED=1|Overnight chat-template switches|Chat-template switches as key=value pairs separated by commas (no spaces), sent to the model's template on every request (llama-server --chat-template-kwargs). 'none' sends nothing; 'auto' uses the kit's value for the Qwen model it ships. Change it only with the model (see docs/MODELS.md). Qwen3.8 maps high to its default xhigh, so medium is the real saving.
NIGHT_SAMPLING|desktop|advanced|sampling|--temp 1.0 --top-p 0.95 --top-k 20 --min-p 0|NIGHT_ENABLED=1|Overnight sampling|llama-server sampling flags, e.g. --temp 0.6 --top-p 0.95 (allowed: --temp --top-p --top-k --min-p --presence-penalty --frequency-penalty --repeat-penalty --repeat-last-n --typical --top-nsigma --xtc-probability --xtc-threshold --dry-multiplier --dry-base --dry-allowed-length --dry-penalty-last-n). 'none' leaves the server defaults; 'auto' uses the kit's values for the Qwen model it ships. Use the model card's recommended values (see docs/MODELS.md).
NIGHT_MTP|desktop|advanced|bool01|1|NIGHT_ENABLED=1|Use the model's MTP head for faster generation|Speculative decoding with the multi-token-prediction head inside the Qwen3.8 files (about 1.4 times faster, same output). Say no for a model without an MTP head, or the server will not start.

#> V100 GPU tier: Tesla V100 SXM2 cards in the desktop (optional, see docs/V100.md)
V100_ENABLED|both|basic|bool01|0||Set up the optional V100 GPU tier|Optional. Tesla V100 cards on SXM2-to-PCIe adapters in the desktop run their own CUDA llama-server on a separate port, with a larger model fully in VRAM, beside the RX 6600 XT server. Read docs/V100.md first: the cards need the right driver, a strong power supply and real cooling.
V100_COUNT|desktop|basic|int:1-4|2|V100_ENABLED=1|Number of V100 cards|How many V100 cards are installed in the desktop. The model is split across them.
V100_VRAM_GB|desktop|basic|choice:16,32|16|V100_ENABLED=1|Memory per V100 card (GB)|16 or 32. nvidia-smi names the card Tesla V100-SXM2-16GB or Tesla V100-SXM2-32GB.
V100_QUANT|desktop|basic|choice:UD-Q4_K_XL,UD-Q5_K_XL,UD-Q6_K_XL,UD-Q8_K_XL|UD-Q4_K_XL|V100_ENABLED=1|V100 model quantization|Larger is better quality but needs more memory. At 128K context: two 16 GB cards take UD-Q4_K_XL, one 32 GB card UD-Q5_K_XL, two 32 GB cards UD-Q6_K_XL. The installer checks the fit against your cards before it downloads anything.
V100_MODEL_ALIAS|both|advanced|alias|qwen3.8-27b|V100_ENABLED=1|V100 model name|The name the V100 server reports and Hermes uses.
V100_MODEL_FILE|desktop|advanced|gguf|Qwen3.8-27B-{V100_QUANT}.gguf|V100_ENABLED=1|V100 model file|File name in the desktop models folder. Derived from the quantization unless you override it.
V100_MODEL_URL|desktop|advanced|url|https://huggingface.co/unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF/resolve/main/Qwen3.8-27B-{V100_QUANT}.gguf|V100_ENABLED=1|V100 model download URL|Check the exact file name on the Hugging Face repo's Files tab if it 404s.
V100_CTX|both|advanced|int:65536-1048576|131072|V100_ENABLED=1|V100 context size (tokens)|Hermes needs at least 65536. A longer context needs more VRAM; the installer checks the fit.
V100_PORT|both|advanced|port|8081|V100_ENABLED=1|V100 model server port|Port of the V100 llama-server on the desktop. Must differ from the model server port; only the laptop may reach it.
V100_MTP|desktop|advanced|bool01|1|V100_ENABLED=1|Use the model's MTP head for faster generation|Speculative decoding with the multi-token-prediction head built into the Qwen3.8-27B files: about 1.3 to 1.5 times faster generation for about 1.3 GB more memory. Used only if the model file has the head (the installer checks). Turn it off if the server runs out of memory.
V100_CHAT_KWARGS|desktop|advanced|kwargs|auto|V100_ENABLED=1|V100 chat-template switches|Chat-template switches as key=value pairs separated by commas (no spaces), sent to the model's template on every request (llama-server --chat-template-kwargs). 'none' sends nothing; 'auto' uses the kit's value for the Qwen model it ships. Change it only with the model (see docs/MODELS.md). 'auto' picks by model family (the 35B-A3B or the 27B).
V100_SAMPLING|desktop|advanced|sampling|auto|V100_ENABLED=1|V100 sampling|llama-server sampling flags, e.g. --temp 0.6 --top-p 0.95 (allowed: --temp --top-p --top-k --min-p --presence-penalty --frequency-penalty --repeat-penalty --repeat-last-n --typical --top-nsigma --xtc-probability --xtc-threshold --dry-multiplier --dry-base --dry-allowed-length --dry-penalty-last-n). 'none' leaves the server defaults; 'auto' uses the kit's values for the Qwen model it ships. Use the model card's recommended values (see docs/MODELS.md). 'auto' picks by model family.
V100_SPLIT_MODE|desktop|advanced|choice:layer,tensor|layer|V100_ENABLED=1|How the model is split over the cards|layer is the safe default and tolerates slow PCIe. tensor is experimental: on the dense 27B it can generate much faster, but it is slower at reading long prompts, uses an unquantized context cache and turns MTP off. Benchmark before you switch (docs/V100.md).
V100_PRIMARY|laptop|advanced|bool01|1|V100_ENABLED=1|Prefer the V100 model on the laptop|Yes puts the V100 model first among the local endpoints (the local profile and the fallback chain). No puts it after the desktop's other model.
V100_DRIVER_MODE|desktop|advanced|choice:TCC,MCDM|TCC|V100_ENABLED=1|V100 driver mode|TCC is the compute-only mode of the NVIDIA data-center driver (no display, no Vulkan; the V100 cannot be a display card anyway). Choose MCDM only if you need the cards inside WSL2.
V100_POWER_LIMIT_W|desktop|advanced|int:0-300|200|V100_ENABLED=1|Power limit per V100 (W)|A V100 SXM2 draws up to 300 W. LLM generation rarely needs more than about 150 W, so 200 W costs little speed and eases the power supply and the cooling. 0 leaves the card's stock limit. The installer never sets more than the card's own default.
V100_CUDA_DIR|desktop|advanced|winpath|C:\llama-cuda|V100_ENABLED=1|Desktop CUDA llama.cpp folder|Where the CUDA build of llama.cpp and its start script live (separate from the Vulkan build in C:\llama).
